2026-10-28|Minimum XOR Path in a Grid

Question:
Given an m × n integer grid, return the minimum possible XOR of all cell values along a path from (0, 0) to (m - 1, n - 1), moving only right or down.

Approach:
1. Define dp[i][j] as the set of all XOR values achievable by paths from (0, 0) to cell (i, j).
2. Initialize dp[0][0] with grid[0][0], since the starting cell is included in the path.
3. For every other cell, consider paths arriving from above and from the left.
4. For each XOR value x from those predecessor cells, calculate x XOR grid[i][j] and add it to dp[i][j].
5. After processing the entire grid, the answer is the smallest value in dp[m - 1][n - 1].

Solution:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int minPathXor(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        int m = grid.size();
        int n = grid[0].size();

        vector<vector<unordered_set<int>>> dp(
            m, vector<unordered_set<int>>(n)
        );

        dp[0][0].insert(grid[0][0]);

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (i == 0 && j == 0) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (i > 0) {
                    for (int x : dp[i - 1][j]) {
                        dp[i][j].insert(x ^ grid[i][j]);
                    }
                }

                if (j > 0) {
                    for (int x : dp[i][j - 1]) {
                        dp[i][j].insert(x ^ grid[i][j]);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return *min_element(
            dp[m - 1][n - 1].begin(),
            dp[m - 1][n - 1].end()
        );
    }
};

Time Complexity: Depends on the number of distinct XOR values reachable at each cell. If at most `S` distinct XOR values are reachable per cell, the expected time complexity is O(m × n × S).

Space Complexity: O(m × n × S), for storing the reachable XOR values at each cell.
